  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={3}^{x+5}-4?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/56f4446a00c12419f722dfd008e3cde785e759aa037fedd47911f0a783f4dfed.png)\n\n- To plot $y={3}^{x+5},$ we shift the graph of $y={3}^{x}$ horizontally by $5$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-5,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/871fe4053ba868f475ded7f0722ab7df20636a4db743f10c7631dede279715f1.png)\n\n- To plot $y={3}^{x+5}-4,$ we shift the graph of $y={3}^{x+5}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-5,1)$ moves to $(-5,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3278d9e3e73ffee57ece6d0abf3343f8315c729e694f111dacef0e759dbe4ab4.png)",
